{
  "description": "GENERATED by scripts/build-gemini-hooks.sh from hooks/hooks.json - do not edit; regenerate with `bash scripts/build-gemini-hooks.sh .`. Gemini CLI reads only the `hooks` object of an extension's hooks/hooks.json (this key is ignored). Events: PreToolUse->BeforeTool, PostToolUse->AfterTool, UserPromptSubmit->BeforeAgent, Stop->AfterAgent, PreCompact->PreCompress; SessionStart, SessionEnd and Notification by name; WorktreeCreate, WorktreeRemove, DirectoryAdded, StopFailure, SubagentStop, TaskCompleted, TeammateIdle, UserPromptExpansion have no Gemini event and are left out, and so are the Claude-only hooks (hooks/permission-approved.sh: the two-phase confirm stages nothing on Gemini). Every command runs the canonical hook through hooks/gemini-wrap.sh, which translates the payload (write_file->Write, replace->Edit, run_shell_command->Bash) and the answer ({decision:deny,reason} / {} / hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext or systemMessage). Timeouts are milliseconds. Claude's onFailure has no Gemini field, so every guard is fail-open here: a guard that crashes or times out lets the action through; policies/*.toml are the hook-free layer. permission-guard.sh answers deny, never ask, on Gemini, and gemini-wrap.sh turns any ask a hook gives into a deny with the same reason (a hook ask hangs a headless run, ADR 004 D8). The GitHub MCP group (Claude matcher mcp__plugin_tamirs-superpowers_github__.*) runs on ^(mcp_github_[A-Za-z0-9_.:-]+)$, the bundled server's tools as Gemini names them (mcp_<server>_<tool>); gemini-wrap.sh rewrites such a call to the Claude name so permission-guard.sh judges it as on Claude Code, and leaves another server's tool under its own name.",
